Why This Is Asked
- Validates per-tenant auditable trails without impacting throughput
- Ensures tamper evidence via signatures and secure storage
- Checks ability to query across tenants with predictable retention

Key Concepts
- Append-only logs, tenant isolation, data integrity, retention strategy
- Tamper detection using MACs or signatures, and secure storage backends
- API design for write/read with minimal surface area and clear semantics

Code Example
```javascript
class AuditLog {
  constructor(storage, signer) {
    this.storage = storage; // per-tenant append-only store
    this.signer = signer;   // cryptographic signing service
  }

  async write(tenant, event) {
    const entry = {
      timestamp: Date.now(),
      tenant,
      event,
      signature: await this.signer.sign(event)
    };
    return await this.storage.append(tenant, entry);
  }

  async query(tenant, since, limit) {
    const entries = await this.storage.read(tenant, since, limit);
    return entries.filter(entry => 
      this.signer.verify(entry.event, entry.signature)
    );
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes a common real-world challenge in Kafka streaming: handling evolving event schemas. It tests understanding of versioned schemas, backward/forward compatibility, data transformation, multi-sink routing with threshold-based logic, and idempotent sinks with per-message deduplication.

## Key Concepts
- **Versioned schemas and backward/forward compatibility**: Handling schema evolution without breaking consumers
- **Data transformation and normalization**: Converting multiple schema versions into a unified structure
- **Idempotent sinks and per-message deduplication**: Ensuring exactly-once semantics using deduplication
- **Multi-sink routing**: Directing events to different destinations based on business logic

## Code Example
```javascript
class VersionedConsumer {
  constructor(threshold, sinks) {
    this.threshold = threshold;
    this.sinks = sinks;
    this.seenIds = new LRUCache(10000);
  }

  process(message) {
    const msgId = message.messageId;
    if (this.seenIds.has(msgId)) return; // skip duplicates

    const record = this.normalize(message);
    const sink = record.value > this.threshold ? this.sinks.alerts : this.sinks.default;
    sink.write(record);
    this.seenIds.add(msgId);
  }

  normalize(msg) {
    const obj = JSON.parse(msg.payload);
    return {
      sensorId: obj.sensorId,
      value: obj.value,
      timestamp: obj.timestamp,
      location: obj.version === 2 ? obj.location : null,
      version: obj.version
    };
  }
}
```

## Canary Test
```javascript
const consumer = new VersionedConsumer(100, { alerts: mockSink(), default: mockSink() });
const v1 = { messageId: '1', payload: JSON.stringify({ version: 1, sensorId: 's1', value: 50, timestamp: 1000 }) };
const v2 = { messageId: '1', payload: JSON.stringify({ version: 2, sensorId: 's2', value: 150, timestamp: 1001, location: 'A' }) };

consumer.process(v1);
consumer.process(v2);
assert(defaultSink.calls === 1);
assert(alertsSink.calls === 1);
```

